929:
(a) The mayor and council hereby find and declare that the unauthorized stationary presence of unattended vehicles in any public way, when said vehicles are either parked, inoperable, or otherwise unlawfully restricting or obstructing the normal flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic, constitutes a nuisance and danger to the general health, safety, and welfare of the citizens of the city.
(b) Any person, at the direction of the chief of police or a duly certified police officer, is hereby authorized to remove and/or impound or have removed and/or impounded at their direction, a vehicle from any public or private way, under any of the circumstances enumerated herein, which circumstances are provided by way of illustration only, and not as a limitation:
(1) When any vehicle is left unattended upon any bridge, viaduct, subway or tunnel, or where such vehicle constitutes an obstruction to traffic;
(2) When a vehicle upon a public way is so disabled as to constitute an obstruction to traffic and the person in charge of the vehicle is for any reason incapacitated to such an extent as to be unable to provide for its custody or removal;
(3) When any vehicle is left unattended upon a street or parked illegally so as to constitute a definite hazard or obstruction to the normal movement of traffic, or left unattended on any public street with engine running or with keys in the ignition switch or locked;
(4) When any vehicle is parked or left standing upon any area or portion of a public street in violation of or contrary to a parking limitation or prohibition established by the chief of police, provided, such area or portion of such public street has been posted with an official sign giving notice both of such limitation or prohibition and of the fact that such area or portion of such street is a "tow-away" area;
(5) When a vehicle is parked in violation of any traffic ordinance and is an obstruction or hazard or potential obstruction or hazard to any lawful function or limits the normal access to a use of any public or private property;
(6) When the driver of such vehicle is taken into custody by the police department for any reason and the vehicle would thereby be left unattended upon a street, highway or restricted parking area, or other public way;
(7) When a vehicle is parked without proper license plates or permits, or with no license plates or permits, or parked with an invalid or expired license permit;
(8) When a vehicle is found parked on or so near to any railroad track as to block the same in any manner;
(9) When any truck-tractor is found to be parked in any metered parking space;
(10) When the driver of any vehicle, or the vehicle which the driver is driving, is reasonably suspected of having been involved in any hit and run accident;
(11) When any vehicle is reasonably suspected of being a stolen vehicle, or parts thereof to be stolen parts;
(12) When any vehicle is parked or stopped in an emergency access lane in violation of or contrary to a parking limitation or prohibition established by city ordinance, provided that such emergency access lane has been posted with an authorized sign giving notice both of such limitation or prohibition and of the fact that such area or portion of such public roadway or private property is a tow-away area.
(c) The owner of any vehicle so removed and impounded shall be required to pay storage and other costs incurred by virtue of such removal and impounding, regardless of the cause of the conduct necessitating such action by the police department.
State Law reference— Disposition of personal property in the custody of law enforcement agencies, O.C.G.A. § 17-5-4; authority to remove vehicles, O.C.G.A. § 40-11-3.

